<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Froben</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1460–<a href="/years/1527">1527</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Johannes Frobenius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Froben was a printer and publisher in Basel and one of the leading printers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. He published the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a>, who lived in his house, including the first published Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> in 1516, and employed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-holbein-the-younger">Hans Holbein the Younger</a> to design title pages and illustrations. His press made Basel a centre of humanist scholarship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-froben.jpg" alt="Johann Froben" width="320" height="492" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Holbein the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_Holbein_the_Younger_-_Johannes_Froben.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
